## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a robust, real-time data layer that stays responsive under network variability, using concurrency primitives and local caching.

## Key Concepts

- Swift concurrency with async/await and TaskGroups
- Local cache using SQLite
- Delta-based merge with per-item versioning
- Conflict resolution and ordering guarantees
- Test strategy for latency, reordering, and memory pressure

## Code Example

```swift
struct Post { let id: String; var version: Int; var content: String }
struct Delta { let id: String; let version: Int; let content: String }

func merge(_ deltas: [Delta], into cache: inout [String: Post]) -> [Post] {
  for d in deltas {
    if var existing = cache[d.id] {
      if d.version > existing.version {
        existing.version = d.version
        existing.content = d.content
        cache[d.id] = existing
      }
    } else {
      cache[d.id] = Post(id: d.id, version: d.version, content: d.content)
    }
  }
  return Array(cache.values)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and mitigate race conditions in the delta merge?
- How would you extend to multi-device sync and server-driven conflict resolution?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates design of a scalable, non-destructive editing pipeline, leveraging Swift concurrency, Core Image, and memory-aware rendering. Also tests undo/redo, testability, and accessibility considerations in a production-like feature.

## Key Concepts
- Swift concurrency and actors for safe state sharing
- Core Image for GPU-accelerated filters
- Non-destructive editing via composable filter passes
- Undo/redo through graph state snapshots
- Memory management with tile-based rendering and lazy recomputation
- Testability: unit tests for graph evaluation; integration tests for export
- Accessibility hooks for UI feedback

## Code Example
```swift
// Skeleton: Filter protocol and graph actor
actor FilterGraph {
  struct Node { var id: String; var filter: CIFilter }
  var nodes: [Node] = []
  func applyAll(base: CIImage) async -> CIImage {
    var current = base
    for n in nodes {
      n.filter.setValue(current, forKey: kCIInputImageKey)
      if let out = n.filter.outputImage {
        current = out
      }
    }
    return current
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to handle very large images on memory-constrained devices?
- What trade-offs exist between tile-based rendering vs full-image compositing, and how would you test them?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates offline-first routing with on-device compute, real-time edge-weight updates, crash safety, and cryptographic authenticity. It also tests SwiftUI/Combine integration and mobile performance optimization.

## Key Concepts
- **Graph algorithms**: A* with incremental weight updates
- **Offline-first data store**: SQLite with write-ahead logging for durability
- **Streaming ingestion**: Combine for processing traffic events
- **Crash safety**: Checkpoints and state persistence for reliable resume
- **Cryptographic verification**: CryptoKit for signed map updates
- **SwiftUI dashboard**: Route visualization with ETA and confidence metrics

## Code Example
```swift
// Graph structure
struct Node: Hashable { let id: String; let coordinate: CLLocationCoordinate2D }
struct Edge { let from: Node.ID; let to: Node.ID; var weight: Double }

class RoutingEngine: ObservableObject {
    @Published var currentRoute: [Node] = []
    @Published var eta: TimeInterval = 0
    @Published var confidence: Double = 0

    private var graph: AdjacencyList<Node.ID, Edge>
    private var checkpoint: RouteCheckpoint?

    // MARK: - A* with Checkpoint Resume
    func computeRoute(from start: Node.ID, to end: Node.ID) {
        let checkpoint = RouteCheckpoint(currentNode: start)
        currentRoute = aStar(graph: graph, start: start, goal: end, checkpoint: checkpoint)
        self.checkpoint = checkpoint
        updateETA()
    }

    func invalidateEdges(_ edges: [Edge.ID]) {
        guard let cp = checkpoint else { return }
        currentRoute = aStar(graph: graph, start: cp.currentNode, goal: goal, checkpoint: cp)
        updateConfidence()
    }

    // MARK: - Combine Traffic Ingestion
    private var cancellables = Set<AnyCancellable>()

    func subscribe(to publisher: AnyPublisher<TrafficEvent, Never>) {
        publisher
            .filter { $0.isSignificant }
            .sink { [weak self] event in
                self?.applyTrafficEvent(event)
            }
            .store(in: &cancellables)
    }

    private func applyTrafficEvent(_ event: TrafficEvent) {
        graph.updateWeight(edgeId: event.edgeId, weight: event.delay)
        invalidateEdges([event.edgeId])
    }

    // MARK: - Map Update Verification
    func verifyAndApplyMapUpdate(_ update: MapUpdate) throws {
        try CryptoKit.verify(update.signature, for: update.data)
        try applyMapUpdate(update)
        checkpoint = nil // Invalidate checkpoint after structural changes
    }
}
```

## Test Plan

| Category | Test Case | Method |
|----------|-----------|--------|
| Routing | A* finds optimal path on static graph | XCTest with known graph |
| Routing | Returns to checkpoint on weight update | Mock weight change, verify partial recomputation |
| Streaming | Traffic events update weights correctly | Combine test publisher, assert graph state |
| Offline | Route computed without network | Disable network, run computeRoute |
| Crash Safety | Resume from checkpoint after simulated crash | Inject crash state, verify recovery |
| Crypto | Reject tampered map updates | Modify signature, expect CryptoKit error |
| UI | Dashboard reflects route/ETA/confidence | ViewInspector snapshot tests |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests CRDT knowledge, offline collaboration, and practical data modeling for cross-device sync. It also evaluates how to bound growth, ensure deterministic reconciliation, and integrate with a common iOS stack.

## Key Concepts
- CRDTs: OR-Set for attachments, observed-remove with vector clocks for changes
- Append-only logs and per-Asset clocks
- Deterministic reconciliation on reconnect
- Realm/SQLite as storage backends
- Lightweight conflict resolver and delta generation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple OR-Set merge sketch (JS for illustration)
function mergeORSet(localAdds, remoteAdds, localRemoves, remoteRemoves) {
  const adds = new Set([...localAdds, ...remoteAdds]);
  const removes = new Set([...localRemoves, ...remoteRemoves]);
  return Array.from(adds).filter(x => !removes.has(x));
}
```

